A||||7||gtsonly||relocation of the MAT sensor||||5||
Z||000000||gtsonly||11-23-2000||12:43 AM||||Has anyone or is there a kit to relocate the MAT sensor to a cooler area of the Fiero, like the airbox? I am to understand that the cooler that sensor is keep the better it will perform which means better performance for the engine. I read this off a Corvette sight which does sell a a relocation kit for the MAT for just that reason, the cooler the sensor the better the performance! Any thoughts?<P>------------------<BR>Chris Hatch <BR>1986 GT w/T-TOPs||||reg||5
Z||000001||terryk||11-23-2000||02:10 AM||||Changes in performace from the MAT/IAT are minimal.||||reg||1
Z||000002||HYPTNOTiSE||11-23-2000||04:50 AM||||It wouldn't take much to move the MAT sensor to the airbox  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  It's already located there. By the way terry cool number on that last post 1111&lt;--Make a wish!||||reg||1
Z||000003||gtsonly||11-23-2000||05:45 AM||||Map sensor, Map sensor!!!  My bad!!!!<P>------------------<BR>Chris Hatch <BR>1986 GT w/T-TOPs||||reg||1
Z||000004||terryk||11-23-2000||12:30 PM||||Map sensor? You might as well wash the car. You'll get more performance from that than moving the map to a cooler location.<P>Just extend the wires and vacuum line.||||reg||12
Z||000005||StuGood||11-23-2000||02:11 PM||||I have a similar question.  I usually keep a big road atlas behind the passenger seat.  Shouldn't I relocate the MAP sensor there?  How much vacuum line and wire will <B>that</B> take   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">?<BR> <BR>FWIW, think my 1987 Cavalier (3.1) has its MAP sensor on the firewall.  I bet moving the MAP to a cooler location wouldn't <B>hurt</B> anything; might give it an easier life since it's plastic.  And it's pretty easy to put it back where it was, if you change your mind.     <p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 11-23-2000).]||||reg||12
Z||000006||HYPTNOTiSE||11-23-2000||02:39 PM||||You want to keep the MAP sensor as close to the intake manifold as possible, The farther it's away the worse your throttle response would be. And keeping the map sensor cold wont do lickety for performance. Like terry said you might as well wash your car or check the air pressure in your tires.||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||11-23-2000||03:26 PM||||anything that makes the map have a longer vac line can be a bad thing. Like Hypno said.<P>The more volume or restriction in that vac line the longer it will take the MAP to see sudden throttle changes. Slow MAP responce = slow ECM responce = worse performance. (restricion is worse than more line volume.)<P>Now, on the 4 cyl the MAP is on the air cleaner. I've shortened my vac line to about 5 inches long. The air cleaner location keeps it pretty cool.<P>On the V6 I understand the MAP is on the right side of the intake manifold. A pretty hot area. Moving it out of there <I>might</I> be good for it thermally but you have to work out how to do that and keep the hose short and make sure the hose can't become pinched.<P>One thing to keep in mind is that the sensor needs manifold vacuum. You can use any available port on the intake. It doesn't have to be the stock one.<P>When extending the electricals you want to solder the connections. The way I do these is to put a section of heat shrink on the wires, solder them, put some RTV silicon on the joint, then shrink the tube over the joint. this seals out water and strengthens the joint to prevent breaking.<P>I say it might be of benifit because the whole engine bay is hot. Just how much cooler the sensor will be in another spot is unkown.<P>You have to pick a new location very carfully. The trunk wall next to the dog bone bracket might be a good spot. ((NOTE if you change how it's mounted you must make sure any vent hole(s) in the top are not blocked.))<P>The issue isn't the plastic case. The case is completely irrelevant. The issue is the pressure transducer and the resistor net inside the sensor. Those want to be kept in a certain temp range. The sensor will also be more accurate if it's temperature is fairly stable.||||reg||1
